h?", "s": 0.05, "e": 4.122}, {"text": "In the ancient world, true purple dye, called Tyrian purple, came from the mucus of tiny predatory sea snails found in the Mediterranean.", "s": 4.302, "e": 14.29}, {"text": "It took roughly 12,000 snails just to produce enough dye for the trim of a single garment.", "s": 14.41, "e": 21.28}, {"text": "Because it smelled like rotting fish during production and cost more than its weight in gold, Roman emperors passed laws banning ordinary citizens from wearing it.", "s": 21.4, "e": 32.439}, {"text": "That is why purple is still associated with wealth and royalty today.", "s": 32.559, "e": 36.874}]}